 "interpretation": [
  {
   "heading": "Taste over actions: no effect, with or without the rest of the agent",
   "body": "On plain DreamerV3, centered taste over actions scores 4.63 against 4.77 (-0.13 ± 0.27 paired by world) and trains slightly lower (5.19 against 5.43 over the last 100k steps). In place of v2's rule mask (v2-taste, uncentered evidence) it scores 5.33: +0.70 ± 0.28 over vanilla, but in training level with v2 without the mask and without taste (v2-norules, not scored). Uncentered, its bias came to follow how often a button was pressed (rank correlation -0.98): placing a table, which v2 needs, ended as disliked as an iron sword it cannot make."
  },
  {
   "heading": "Taste on the manager cost the next agent about two achievements (masked runs)",
   "body": "Next-taste locked the manager onto one goal, place table, at its first update (6.7k steps) and held it to the end: only a chosen goal gets evidence, centering over the chosen goals leaves the winner's evidence near zero, and taste outweighed the critic's spread between goals 15 to 300 times over. Against the same agent with taste switched off it is -1.92 ± 0.41 over 60 worlds; it never made a stone tool, while the control made the stone sword in half its episodes on worlds 0-29. Both runs use the rule mask, so neither is comparable with knowledge-free agents. docs/taste-prior.md, \"Why taste hurt\"."
  },
  {
   "heading": "What would settle the rest",
   "body": "Every taste result is one seed. If taste is kept: put it on the critic's scale (units of the critic's own spread between goals), let evidence reach goals the manager did not choose or decay back to neutral, treat \"no goal\" like the other goals, and test it without the rule mask."
  }
 ],
